definerT -- EVERY OCCURRENCE OF A RULE ANSWERS THE REGISTRY'S ENTRY AS ITS DEFINER (SEQ 216, 2026-09-27).

RESPELLED 2026-09-30 (stroke 4.4a): definersOf now asks instanceRule(), and definingRule() is deleted. The rows held
row for row. instanceRule() reads ruleOf, not the first child's parent, so this fixture no longer witnesses the
child-parenting invariant described below -- see the stroke's coverage finding.
WATCH GIVEN BACK 2026-09-30 (SEQ 233): definersOf also prints CHILDPARENTS -- each child of the shared list against
the list's owner -- and pop.sh row DF-2 pins it. H7: compile()'s old parent write restored turns it red.

A rule's child list is SHARED by every place the rule is used, and definingRule() reads the owner of
that list off the first child's parent -- so the invariant is that shared children stay parented to the
REGISTRY's entry. Two loops broke it, both added in 5f24cf3 (2026-09-14): compile() and setParseWalk
each wrote `parent = field` on every child before recursing into it, so definingRule() drifted to
whichever occurrence was compiled or walked last. Under ruling A every rule body asks its definer for
its parse method (runLeafParse), and after a second parser() the drift landed on a Token-side face that
setParse never installed: "no parse method is installed on the defining rule" on 160 of 171 drives.

definersOf(name) walks the Grokking registry and every rule's terms, finds each occurrence of the tag,
and counts how many answer the registry's entry. It prints the counts unconditionally. All three
readings must show others=0.
H7, measured on a426178 (the loops still writing): the second reading's others is non-zero.
